# Tracked, time-boxed exceptions for specific CVEs someone has explicitly
# triaged and accepted (not a blanket "no fix yet" bypass - the informational
# scan in the pipeline already surfaces those for visibility without needing
# an entry here). Format: CVE-ID exp:YYYY-MM-DD [reason]
#
# The exp: date is load-bearing: once it passes, Trivy stops honoring the
# entry and the CVE starts hard-blocking the pipeline again, forcing a
# re-triage rather than a silent permanent bypass.
#
# Example (not currently active - no CVEs have been triaged/accepted yet):
# CVE-2024-XXXXX exp:2026-10-01 accepted: no upstream fix, low exploitability in our usage, revisit at expiry

# Both of these are embedded inside python:3.11-slim's own CPython
# distribution (ensurepip's bundled setuptools wheel, and a vendored
# msgpack inside pip's own internals) - static artifacts baked into the
# Python release itself, not something `pip install --upgrade` touches (we
# already upgraded pip/setuptools/wheel in the Dockerfile; the site-packages
# copies Trivy separately reports are clean). Our Flask app never invokes
# ensurepip or pip's internal vendored code paths at runtime, so there is no
# reachable attack surface here. Revisit at expiry: either a newer
# python:3.11-slim patch release ships an updated ensurepip bundle, or strip
# ensurepip/pip's vendor directory from the final image as a follow-up
# hardening step.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-10-30 msgpack embedded in pip's vendored deps inside python:3.11-slim - not reachable at runtime, revisit at expiry
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-10-30 setuptools embedded in ensurepip's bundled wheel inside python:3.11-slim - not reachable at runtime, revisit at expiry
